.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt PROVIDER 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm provider
.Nd OpenSSL operation implementation providers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
#include <openssl/provider.h>
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Ss General
This page contains information useful to provider authors.
.Pp
A
.Em provider ,
in OpenSSL terms, is a unit of code that provides one or more
implementations for various operations for diverse algorithms that one
might want to perform.
.Pp
An
.Em operation
is something one wants to do, such as encryption and decryption, key
derivation, MAC calculation, signing and verification, etc.
.Pp
An
.Em algorithm
is a named method to perform an operation.
Very often, the algorithms revolve around cryptographic operations, but
may also revolve around other types of operation, such as managing
certain types of objects.
.Pp
See
.Xr crypto 7
for further details.
.Ss Provider
A
.Em provider
offers an initialization function, as a set of base functions in the
form of an
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
array, and by extension, a set of
.Xr OSSL_ALGORITHM 3 Ns s
(see
.Xr openssl-core.h 7 Ns ).
It may be a dynamically loadable module, or may be built-in, in OpenSSL
libraries or in the application.
If it's a dynamically loadable module, the initialization function must
be named
.Qo Li OSSL_provider_init Qc
and must be exported.
If it's built-in, the initialization function may have any name.
.Pp
The initialization function must have the following signature:
.Bd -literal
 int NAME(const OSSL_CORE_HANDLE *handle,
          const OSSL_DISPATCH *in, const OSSL_DISPATCH **out,
          void **provctx);
.Ed
.Pp
.Em handle
is the OpenSSL library object for the provider, and works as a handle
for everything the OpenSSL libraries need to know about the provider.
For the provider itself, it is passed to some of the functions given in
the dispatch array
.Em in .
.Pp
.Em in
is a dispatch array of base functions offered by the OpenSSL libraries,
and the available functions are further described in
.Xr provider-base 7 .
.Pp
.Em *out
must be assigned a dispatch array of base functions that the provider
offers to the OpenSSL libraries.
The functions that may be offered are further described in
.Xr provider-base 7 ,
and they are the central means of communication between the OpenSSL
libraries and the provider.
.Pp
.Em *provctx
should be assigned a provider specific context to allow the provider
multiple simultaneous uses.
This pointer will be passed to various operation functions offered by
the provider.
.Pp
Note that the provider will not be made available for applications to
use until the initialization function has completed and returned
successfully.
.Pp
One of the functions the provider offers to the OpenSSL libraries is the
central mechanism for the OpenSSL libraries to get access to operation
implementations for diverse algorithms.
Its referred to with the number
.Sy OSSL_FUNC_PROVIDER_QUERY_OPERATION
and has the following signature:
.Bd -literal
 const OSSL_ALGORITHM *provider_query_operation(void *provctx,
                                                int operation_id,
                                                const int *no_store);
.Ed
.Pp
.Em provctx
is the provider specific context that was passed back by the
initialization function.
.Pp
.Em operation_id
is an operation identity (see
.Sx Operations
below).
.Pp
.Em no_store
is a flag back to the OpenSSL libraries which, when nonzero, signifies
that the OpenSSL libraries will not store a reference to the returned
data in their internal store of implementations.
.Pp
The returned
.Xr OSSL_ALGORITHM 3
is the foundation of any OpenSSL library API that uses providers for
their implementation, most commonly in the
.Em fetching
type of functions (see
.Xr crypto 7 Ns ).
.Ss Operations
Operations are referred to with numbers, via macros with names starting
with
.Qo Li OSSL_OP_ Qc .
.Pp
With each operation comes a set of defined function types that a
provider may or may not offer, depending on its needs.
.Pp
Currently available operations are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Digests
In the OpenSSL libraries, the corresponding method object is
.Sy EVP_MD .
The number for this operation is
.Sy OSSL_OP_DIGEST .
The functions the provider can offer are described in
.Xr provider-digest 7 .
.It Symmetric ciphers
In the OpenSSL libraries, the corresponding method object is
.Sy EVP_CIPHER .
The number for this operation is
.Sy OSSL_OP_CIPHER .
The functions the provider can offer are described in
.Xr provider-cipher 7 .
.It Message Authentication Code (MAC)
In the OpenSSL libraries, the corresponding method object is
.Sy EVP_MAC .
The number for this operation is
.Sy OSSL_OP_MAC .
The functions the provider can offer are described in
.Xr provider-mac 7 .
.It Key Derivation Function (KDF)
In the OpenSSL libraries, the corresponding method object is
.Sy EVP_KDF .
The number for this operation is
.Sy OSSL_OP_KDF .
The functions the provider can offer are described in
.Xr provider-kdf 7 .
.It Key Exchange
In the OpenSSL libraries, the corresponding method object is
.Sy EVP_KEYEXCH .
The number for this operation is
.Sy OSSL_OP_KEYEXCH .
The functions the provider can offer are described in
.Xr provider-keyexch 7 .
.It Asymmetric Ciphers
In the OpenSSL libraries, the corresponding method object is
.Sy EVP_ASYM_CIPHER .
The number for this operation is
.Sy OSSL_OP_ASYM_CIPHER .
The functions the provider can offer are described in
.Xr provider-asym_cipher 7 .
.It Asymmetric Key Encapsulation
In the OpenSSL libraries, the corresponding method object is
.Sy EVP_KEM .
The number for this operation is
.Sy OSSL_OP_KEM .
The functions the provider can offer are described in
.Xr provider-kem 7 .
.It Encoding
In the OpenSSL libraries, the corresponding method object is
.Sy OSSL_ENCODER .
The number for this operation is
.Sy OSSL_OP_ENCODER .
The functions the provider can offer are described in
.Xr provider-encoder 7 .
.It Decoding
In the OpenSSL libraries, the corresponding method object is
.Sy OSSL_DECODER .
The number for this operation is
.Sy OSSL_OP_DECODER .
The functions the provider can offer are described in
.Xr provider-decoder 7 .
.It Random Number Generation
The number for this operation is
.Sy OSSL_OP_RAND .
The functions the provider can offer for random number generation are
described in
.Xr provider-rand 7 .
.It Key Management
The number for this operation is
.Sy OSSL_OP_KEYMGMT .
The functions the provider can offer for key management are described in
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7 .
.It Signing and Signature Verification
The number for this operation is
.Sy OSSL_OP_SIGNATURE .
The functions the provider can offer for digital signatures are
described in
.Xr provider-signature 7 .
.It Store Management
The number for this operation is
.Sy OSSL_OP_STORE .
The functions the provider can offer for store management are described
in
.Xr provider-storemgmt 7 .
.El
.Pp
.Em Algorithm naming
.Pp
Algorithm names are case insensitive.
Any particular algorithm can have multiple aliases associated with it.
The canonical OpenSSL naming scheme follows this format:
.Pp
ALGNAME[VERSION?][-SUBNAME[VERSION?]?][-SIZE?][-MODE?]
.Pp
VERSION is only present if there are multiple versions of an algorithm
(e.g.
MD2, MD4, MD5). It may be omitted if there is only one version.
.Pp
SUBNAME may be present where multiple algorithms are combined together,
e.g.
MD5-SHA1.
.Pp
SIZE is only present if multiple versions of an algorithm exist with
different sizes (e.g.
AES-128-CBC, AES-256-CBC)
.Pp
MODE is only present where applicable.
.Pp
Other aliases may exist for example where standards bodies or common
practice use alternative names or names that OpenSSL has used
historically.
.Pp
.Em Provider dependencies
.Pp
Providers may depend for their proper operation on the availability of
(functionality implemented in) other providers.
As there is no mechanism to express such dependencies towards the
OpenSSL core, provider authors must take care that such dependencies are
either completely avoided or made visible to users, e.g., by
documentation and/or defensive programming, e.g., outputting error
messages if required external dependencies are not available, e.g., when
no provider implementing the required functionality has been activated.
In particular, provider initialization should not depend on other
providers already having been initialized.
.Pp
.Em Note on naming clashes
.Pp
It is possible to register the same algorithm name from within different
providers.
Users should note that if no property query is specified, or more than
one implementation matches the property query then it is unspecified
which implementation of a particular algorithm will be returned.
Such naming clashes may also occur if algorithms only differ in
capitalization as
.Sx Algorithm naming
is case insensitive.
.Sh OPENSSL PROVIDERS
OpenSSL provides a number of its own providers.
These are the default, base, fips, legacy and null providers.
See
.Xr crypto 7
for an overview of these providers.
